## HEAVY (other seats and reviews share this machine and ONE lock)
A heavy job = `dotnet restore`, `dotnet build`, `dotnet test`. **Every heavy job goes through `<lane>/gate/heavy.py`, in the foreground, ONE at a time; you never start `dotnet` any other way.** What the runner does (kit B1813's runner, the second review's rule): (1) reads load1 FIRST and waits while it is 7 or more (at most 30 minutes, then `BLOCKED: load`); (2) THEN takes the wave's lock by `mkdir` of EXACTLY `/Volumes/Datasec_DEV/!CODING/Datasec/OXPd_2/.tools/wave1-heavy.lock.d` and writes `owner.txt` inside it; (3) runs the ONE command under a time bound (900 s); (4) releases the lock by MOVING the folder to `<lane>/_quarantine/locks/` (never `rm`, never `rmdir`). It sets the three settings that stop build processes outliving a job (`MSBUILDDISABLENODEREUSE=1`, `DOTNET_CLI_USE_MSBUILD_SERVER=0`, `UseSharedCompilation=false`), the closed local proxy, `DOTNET_ROOT` and `PATH` for the project's own SDK, the project's own package cache, and a `HOME` under `<lane>/dotnet-home`. A lock held by another seat for more than 30 minutes ends the runner with `BLOCKED: lock held <its owner line>`: that line is your last line; an `owner.txt` is never yours to break. A wait is not a finding. (The drafter ran NO job through this runner for this kit; kit B1813's seat and drafter ran it unchanged, RELAYED.)

